[Chapter "Key ideas: Why the Queen's Gambit"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "ideas"]

{The Queen's Gambit fights for the center from the side. Black answers 2.c4 with 2...e6 in a third of your games, 2...c6 in a quarter and 2...dxc4 in about one in six. This course covers 2...e6; the Slav and Queen's Gambit Accepted courses cover the other two. Each board below shows one idea behind White's play.}
*

[Chapter "Pressure on d5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "1...d5"]

{After 1.d4 d5, Black's pawn on d5 stops e4 for now. So White attacks d5 from the side with the c-pawn. The pawn on d4 is safe: the queen guards it.}
1. d4 d5 {[%cal Rd5e4]}
2. c4 {[%cal Gc4d5] The Queen's Gambit. A wing pawn attacks d5, Black's only center pawn.} e6 {[%cal Ge6d5] [%csl Yc8] The Queen's Gambit Declined: a pawn holds d5. The price is the c8 bishop, shut in behind the pawn on e6.}
3. Nc3 {[%cal Gc3d5] A second attacker on d5.} Nf6 {[%cal Gf6d5] Black guards d5 again and stops e4. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Not a real gambit"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "2...dxc4"]

{If Black takes the pawn (the Queen's Gambit Accepted), Black's d-pawn leaves the center. White doesn't need to hurry: the f1 bishop wins the pawn back. Trying to keep it with ...b5 runs into a tactic on the long diagonal (see Typical tactics).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 {[%cal Gf1c4] [%csl Yc4]}
3. e3 {[%cal Gf1c4] Opens the diagonal of the f1 bishop.} Nf6 {The most common reply.}
4. Bxc4 {[%csl Yd4] The pawn is back. White has a pawn on d4, and Black's d-pawn is gone. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Keep the c8 bishop quiet"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "3...Nf6"]

{After 1.d4 d5 2.c4 e6 3.Nc3 Nf6, this course takes on d5 at once, before Black can take on c4. Black takes back with the pawn in three games out of four, and that opens the diagonal of the c8 bishop, Black's problem piece. White's next moves keep it off f5, its most natural square.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 {[%csl Yc8]}
4. cxd5 {[%cal Gc4d5] The Exchange Variation.} exd5 {[%cal Gc8f5] The c8 bishop can come out now.}
5. Bg5 {[%cal Gg5f6,Rf6d5] Pins the knight that guards d5.} c6
6. Qc2 {[%cal Gc2f5] The queen covers f5, so the bishop can't go there yet. Later Bd3 covers it too. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Plans in the Exchange Variation"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "ideas"]

{The Exchange Variation gives almost the same pawn structure in every game, so the plans repeat too. The boards below start from the main position of this course: 1.d4 d5 2.c4 e6 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.cxd5 exd5 5.Bg5 c6 6.Qc2 Be7 7.e3 O-O 8.Bd3 Nbd7 9.Nge2 Re8 10.O-O Nf8. Stockfish rates it as equal (+0.2). Each board shows one plan with the few moves that carry it out.}
*

[Chapter "The minority attack"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "10...Nf8"]

{White's two queenside pawns (a and b) attack Black's three (a, b and c). The rook goes to b1, then the b-pawn advances to b4 and b5. When it takes on c6, Black is left with a weak pawn on c6 or d5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 c6 6. Qc2 Be7 7. e3 O-O 8. Bd3 Nbd7 9. Nge2 Re8 10. O-O Nf8 {[%cal Gb2b4,Gb4b5] [%csl Yc6]}
11. Rab1 {[%cal Gb1b4] The rook stands behind the b-pawn.} a5 {[%cal Ra5b4] Black stops b4 for now: 12.b4? axb4 just loses a pawn, since b4 is guarded by the e7 bishop (Stockfish −1.2).}
12. a3 {Prepares b4 again.} g6
13. b4 axb4
14. axb4 {[%cal Gb4b5] [%csl Yc6] Next comes b5. After b5 and bxc6, Black is left with the weak pawn. The next board shows Black's best answer.} *

[Chapter "Black's answer: ...b5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "14.axb4"]

{The same position after 11.Rab1 a5 12.a3 g6 13.b4 axb4 14.axb4. Here Stockfish's first choice is to block White's b-pawn with ...b5. Black has other ways to meet the minority attack too: see the Key ideas page "Meeting the Exchange".}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 c6 6. Qc2 Be7 7. e3 O-O 8. Bd3 Nbd7 9. Nge2 Re8 10. O-O Nf8 11. Rab1 a5 12. a3 g6 13. b4 axb4 14. axb4
14... b5 {[%csl Yc5,Yc6] White's b-pawn can't advance any more. The price: the c6 pawn is fixed on the half-open c-file, and no black pawn can guard c5 now.}
15. Ra1 {[%cal Ga1a8] White's rook takes the open a-file instead. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "f3 and e4"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "10...Nf8"]

{White's other plan uses the knight on e2, not f3, which leaves the f-pawn free. f3 takes the e4 square away from Black's knights and prepares e4, which opens the center.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 c6 6. Qc2 Be7 7. e3 O-O 8. Bd3 Nbd7 9. Nge2 Re8 10. O-O Nf8 {[%cal Gf2f3,Ge3e4] [%csl Ye4]}
11. f3 {[%csl Ye4] No black knight can come to e4 now.} g6
12. Rae1 {[%cal Ge1e4,Ge3e4] The rook backs up the e-pawn. Push e4 only when your pieces are ready: played too early, Black takes on e4 and Stockfish then slightly prefers Black.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Three pawn structures"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Three pawn structures come up again and again in the Queen's Gambit, and each one tells both sides where to play. The first comes from this course, the other two from the Queen's Gambit Accepted and the Slav. Each board shows how the structure arises and what White does with it.}
*

[Chapter "The Carlsbad structure"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "both"]
[Diagram "3...c6"]

{Named after the Carlsbad tournament of 1923. It comes from the Exchange Variation, from the Slav with 4...e6, and from this position: 1.d4 d5 2.c4 e6 3.Nc3 c6. The trade on d5 creates it.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 c6 {[%cal Gc4d5]}
4. cxd5 {White gives up the c-pawn for Black's e-pawn.} exd5 {Now White has the half-open c-file and Black the half-open e-file. The c8 bishop's diagonal is open too.}
5. Bf4 {With no knight on f6 to pin, the bishop goes to f4.} Nf6
6. e3 {[%cal Gb2b4,Gb4b5] [%csl Yc6,Yd5] The Carlsbad structure: White's pawns on d4 and e3 face Black's on c6 and d5. White's two queenside pawns can attack Black's three with b4 and b5, to leave a weak pawn on c6 or d5: the minority attack (see Plans in the Exchange Variation). Black looks for play in the center and on the kingside. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "The isolated queen's pawn"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "both"]
[Diagram "6.O-O"]

{From the Queen's Gambit Accepted: 1.d4 d5 2.c4 dxc4 3.e3 Nf6 4.Bxc4 e6 5.Nf3 c5 6.O-O. If Black takes on d4 and White takes back with the e-pawn, White's d-pawn has no pawn beside it, so only pieces can guard it. In return White gets open lines, more space and the e5 square for a knight.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 Nf6 4. Bxc4 e6 5. Nf3 c5 6. O-O {[%cal Rc5d4]}
6... cxd4 7. exd4 {[%csl Yd4] The isolated queen's pawn. Keep pieces on the board: in an endgame the pawn is only a weakness.} Be7
8. Nc3 {Develops and watches d5, the square in front of the pawn.} O-O
9. Re1 {[%cal Ge1e5] The rook backs up a knight on e5.} a6
10. Ne5 {[%cal Gd4e5] The knight takes the square the pawn gives it. Next the c1 bishop comes out. With pieces on the board, White can attack on the kingside or push d5 to open lines. Stockfish: +0.4, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "The Slav center"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "both"]
[Diagram "8...O-O"]

{From the Slav: 1.d4 d5 2.c4 c6 3.Nf3 Nf6 4.Nc3 dxc4 5.a4 Bf5 6.e3 e6 7.Bxc4 Bb4 8.O-O O-O. Black's pawns on c6 and e6 make a solid wall. White has won the c4 pawn back, and a4 stopped ...b5. Now White plays for e4, to get the bigger center.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 dxc4 5. a4 Bf5 6. e3 e6 7. Bxc4 Bb4 8. O-O O-O {[%cal Ge3e4]}
9. Qe2 {[%cal Ge3e4,Gf1d1] Prepares e4 and frees d1 for a rook.} Nbd7
10. e4 {[%csl Yd4,Ye4] White's pawns on d4 and e4 take more space than Black's on c6 and e6, and e4 hits the bishop.} Bg6 {The bishop steps back. Stockfish: +0.4, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Typical tactics"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Four tactics to know as White. Three of them punish Black for leaving something without a guard: the rook on a8, the pawn on b7 and the bishop on c8. The last is a trap for White, so its board shows what not to do.}
*

[Chapter "Qf3 and the long diagonal"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "5.axb5"]

{From the Queen's Gambit Accepted: 1.d4 d5 2.c4 dxc4 3.e3 b5 4.a4 c6 5.axb5. Almost one Accepted player in five tries to keep the extra pawn with 3...b5. Now the natural recapture opens the long diagonal from f3 to a8.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 b5 4. a4 c6 5. axb5 {[%cal Rf3a8]}
5... cxb5? {[%cal Gd1f3,Gf3a8] Opens the diagonal. Nothing stands between f3 and the rook on a8.}
6. Qf3 {[%cal Gf3a8] Hits the rook. Black can't save it without losing a piece.} Nc6 {The best try: the knight blocks.}
7. Qxc6+ {Nothing guards the knight, so the queen takes it with check.} Bd7
8. Qa6 {White has won a knight for a pawn. Stockfish: +5.6, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Qb3 against b7"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "6.e3"]

{From the Exchange Variation: 1.d4 d5 2.c4 e6 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.cxd5 exd5 5.Bg5 Be7 6.e3. When Black's light-squared bishop leaves c8, nothing guards b7. A queen on b3 hits it, often together with a second target. The same idea comes up in the Slav and the Accepted.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 Be7 6. e3 {[%cal Gc8b7]}
6... Bf5?! {[%csl Yb7] The bishop comes out, but b7 is left alone.}
7. Bxf6 {Removes the knight that guards d5.} Bxf6
8. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7,Gb3d5] Hits b7 and d5 at the same time.} c6 {Black guards d5, and b7 falls.}
9. Qxb7 {White has won a pawn. Stockfish: +1.0, clearly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Nxd5 when c8 is loose"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "7.e3"]

{From the Exchange Variation: 1.d4 d5 2.c4 e6 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.cxd5 exd5 5.Bg5 c6 6.Qc2 Be7 7.e3. White's queen and Black's c8 bishop stand on the same file, with only the knight on c3 and the pawn on c6 between them. While Black's knight is still on b8, only the queen on d8 guards c8. If the queen leaves d8, Nxd5 wins a pawn, because cxd5 would open the c-file.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 c6 6. Qc2 Be7 7. e3 {[%cal Gc2c8]}
7... Nh5? {Makes way for ...Bxg5, to trade White's good bishop.}
8. Bxe7 Qxe7? {The natural recapture, but now nothing guards c8. 8...Kxe7 keeps the queen on d8, and Black loses no material (Stockfish +1.0, clearly better for White).}
9. Nxd5! {[%cal Gc2c8] Takes a pawn, because the c6 pawn can't take back safely.} cxd5? {9...Qd6 is better, but Black stays a pawn down (Stockfish +3.1, winning for White).}
10. Qxc8+ {The c-file is open, and the queen takes the bishop with check. Stockfish: +5.5, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Don't take on d5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "5...Nbd7"]

{From the Exchange Variation: 1.d4 d5 2.c4 e6 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.cxd5 exd5 5.Bg5 Nbd7. The pawn on d5 looks free, because the knight on f6 is pinned. It is a trap, the Elephant trap, and one White player in five falls for it. Play 6.e3 and develop.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 Nbd7 {[%cal Rg5f6,Rf6d5] [%csl Yd5]}
6. Nxd5?? {Grabs the pawn.} Nxd5! {The pin isn't real: Black gives up the queen.}
7. Bxd8 Bb4+ {[%cal Gb4e1] Check, and White can only block with the queen.}
8. Qd2 Bxd2+ {Black takes the queen back. 8...Kxd8 is even stronger.}
9. Kxd2 Kxd8 {Black has won a knight for a pawn. Stockfish: −5.1, winning for Black.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Why the Queen's Gambit Declined"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Black's setup in this course is the Tartakower Variation, also called the Tartakower–Makogonov–Bondarevsky system: ...Nf6, ...Be7, castle, ...h6, then ...b6 and ...Bb7. It was a regular guest in the world championship matches between Anatoly Karpov and Garry Kasparov, and nearly every game of the 1927 match between José Raúl Capablanca and Alexander Alekhine began with the Queen's Gambit Declined. Each board below shows one step of the setup and why Black plays it.}
*

[Chapter "A pawn holds d5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "2.c4"]

{White's c-pawn attacks d5. Black can take it, as in the Queen's Gambit Accepted, or hold d5. The Declined holds it with the e-pawn, so a pawn is ready to take back if White takes on d5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 {[%cal Rc4d5]}
2... e6 {[%cal Ge6d5] [%csl Yc8] The pawn on e6 guards d5. The price: the c8 bishop is shut in.}
3. Nc3 Nf6 {[%cal Gf6d5] Guards d5 again and prepares to castle.}
4. cxd5 {The Exchange Variation, in about one game in six here.} exd5 {[%cal Gc8f5] Black still has a pawn on d5, and the trade has opened the c8 bishop's diagonal.} *

[Chapter "Break the pin, then castle"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "4.Bg5"]

{White's main line, 4.Bg5, in about a third of games, pins the knight that guards d5. One quiet move breaks the pin, and the king is safe a move later.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 {[%cal Rg5f6,Rf6d5]}
4... Be7 {[%cal Ge7f6] The bishop stands between the knight and the queen, so the knight can move again.}
5. e3 O-O {The king is safe.}
6. Nf3 {5.Nf3 O-O 6.e3 reaches the same position. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "...h6 and ...b6"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "6.Nf3"]

{The position after 6.Nf3, and the two moves that make the Tartakower. ...h6 asks White's bishop to decide. ...b6 opens a long diagonal for the c8 bishop, so it is no longer shut in, and it supports ...c5, Black's freeing break.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 {[%csl Yc8]}
6... h6 {[%cal Rh6g5] Puts the question to the bishop. 7.Bxf6 Bxf6 gives Black the bishop pair (Stockfish +0.1, equal). The pawn on h6 also gives Black's king an escape square later.}
7. Bh4 b6 {[%cal Gc8b7,Gc7c5] The Tartakower.}
8. Bd3 Bb7 {[%cal Gb7d5] The bishop is out, on the long diagonal behind the pawn on d5. Next come ...Nbd7 and ...c5: see Plans in the Tartakower. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Plans in the Tartakower"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "ideas"]

{The Tartakower comes after 1.d4 d5 2.c4 e6 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.Bg5 Be7 5.e3 O-O 6.Nf3 h6 7.Bh4 b6. Black has less space, so the plan is to trade pieces, bring out the c8 bishop and free the game with ...c5. Stockfish rates the position as equal (+0.1). Each board shows one way to carry out the plan.}
*

[Chapter "Trade on d5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "8.cxd5"]

{Four White players in ten take on d5 at once with 8.cxd5. Take back with the knight: it offers trades, and every trade gives Black's pieces more room. Once two pairs of minor pieces are gone, the c8 bishop is free too.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 b6 8. cxd5 {[%cal Gf6d5]}
8... Nxd5 {[%cal Ge7h4] The knight takes back, and the e7 bishop now attacks White's bishop on h4.}
9. Bxe7 Qxe7 {One pair of bishops is gone.}
10. Nxd5 exd5 {[%cal Gc8h3] [%csl Yd5] A pair of knights is gone too. The c8 bishop's diagonal is open, and Black has the half-open e-file. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "Take on c4 after Bd3"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "8.Bd3"]

{About one White player in four plays 8.Bd3 instead. Finish your development first, then take on c4: White's bishop has already moved once, so taking back costs it a second move. Then ...c5 hits White's center.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 b6 8. Bd3 {[%cal Gd5c4]}
8... Bb7 {[%cal Gb7d5] The bishop goes to the long diagonal, behind the pawn on d5.}
9. O-O Nbd7 {The last minor piece comes out.}
10. Rc1 {The most common move.} dxc4 {Now the bishop on d3 has to move a second time.}
11. Bxc4 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4,Gb7f3] The freeing break. With the d5 pawn gone, the b7 bishop looks down the long diagonal. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "...Ne4 trades pieces"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "11.Rfd1"]

{This position comes from 8.Bd3 Bb7 9.O-O Nbd7 10.Qe2 c5 11.Rfd1. The knight jumps to e4: it hits the knight on c3, and it lets the e7 bishop attack White's bishop on h4. Two pairs of minor pieces come off, and with fewer pieces on the board, Black's smaller space matters less.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 b6 8. Bd3 Bb7 9. O-O Nbd7 10. Qe2 c5 11. Rfd1
11... Ne4 {[%cal Ge4c3,Ge7h4] The knight offers a trade, and the e7 bishop now attacks h4.}
12. Bxe7 Qxe7 {One pair of bishops is gone.}
13. cxd5 Nxc3 {[%cal Gc3e2,Gc3d1] Before taking back on d5, Black trades knights. The knight on c3 attacks White's queen and rook, so White has to take it.}
14. bxc3 exd5 {Two pairs of minor pieces are gone, and Black's pieces have room. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: The hanging pawns"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Hanging pawns are two pawns side by side, here on c5 and d5, with no pawns of their own on the files next to them. Black often gets them in the Tartakower, after the trades on d5 and ...c5. They can't guard each other, so Black's pieces must, but they control the center and can advance. Each board shows one way to handle them.}
*

[Chapter "...c5 and ...bxc5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "12.Bd3"]

{This position comes from the Tartakower after 8.cxd5 Nxd5 9.Bxe7 Qxe7 10.Nxd5 exd5 11.Rc1 Be6 12.Bd3. The bishop stands on e6, not b7: with the pawn fixed on d5, a bishop on b7 would bite on its own pawn, while on e6 it guards d5. Now ...c5 hits White's center.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 b6 8. cxd5 Nxd5 9. Bxe7 Qxe7 10. Nxd5 exd5 11. Rc1 Be6 12. Bd3 {[%cal Ge6d5]}
12... c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4.}
13. dxc5 bxc5 {[%csl Yb4,Yc4,Yd4,Ye4] Take back with the pawn. The pawns on c5 and d5 control b4, c4, d4 and e4. Taking back with a piece would leave the d5 pawn isolated, and 13...Qxc5?? even loses the queen to 14.Rxc5. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "Pieces guard the pawns"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "13...bxc5"]

{The same position after 13...bxc5. The knight goes to f6 by way of d7, where it guards d5. A rook takes the half-open b-file, and ...a5 gains space on the queenside.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 b6 8. cxd5 Nxd5 9. Bxe7 Qxe7 10. Nxd5 exd5 11. Rc1 Be6 12. Bd3 c5 13. dxc5 bxc5 {[%cal Gb8d7,Gd7f6,Ga8b8]}
14. O-O Nd7 {The knight heads for f6.}
15. b3 a5 {Gains space on the queenside.}
16. h3 Nf6 {[%cal Gf6d5] The knight guards d5, beside the bishop on e6.}
17. Re1 Rab8 {[%cal Gb8b3] The rook takes the half-open b-file and hits b3. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "...d4 against e4"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "14...Nd7"]

{The same structure after 14.O-O Nd7. Push a hanging pawn only with a purpose. When White attacks d5 with e4, ...d4 is the answer: the pawn becomes a passed pawn, and the pawn on c5 guards it.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 b6 8. cxd5 Nxd5 9. Bxe7 Qxe7 10. Nxd5 exd5 11. Rc1 Be6 12. Bd3 c5 13. dxc5 bxc5 14. O-O Nd7
15. e4 {[%cal Re4d5] Attacks d5.} d4 {[%csl Yd4] A passed pawn, guarded by c5. White's bishop on d3 now bites on its own pawn on e4.}
16. b3 Rfd8 {The rook comes to the d-file, behind the passed pawn. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "...c4 gains space"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "13.Rc1"]

{In the main line, after 10...exd5 11.Bd3 Be6 12.O-O c5 13.Rc1, White has not taken on c5. Here ...c4 pushes with a purpose: it drives White's bishop back and gains space on the queenside. The pawns are no longer hanging, since d5 guards c4.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 b6 8. cxd5 Nxd5 9. Bxe7 Qxe7 10. Nxd5 exd5 11. Bd3 Be6 12. O-O c5 13. Rc1
13... c4 {[%cal Gc4d3] Hits the bishop.}
14. Bb1 Nc6 {The last minor piece comes out. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Tricks and traps"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Four tactics to know. In the first, White grabs a pawn and loses a piece. In the other three, Black takes back with the wrong piece and pays for it. Each board starts just before the mistake.}
*

[Chapter "The Elephant trap"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "5...Nbd7"]

{In the Exchange Variation, after 4.cxd5 exd5 5.Bg5 Nbd7, the pawn on d5 looks free, because the knight on f6 is pinned to the queen. About one White player in five grabs it. The pin isn't real: Black gives up the queen and wins more back.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 Nbd7 {[%csl Yd5] [%cal Rg5f6]}
6. Nxd5?? {Grabs the pawn, and loses a piece.} Nxd5! {Gives up the queen.}
7. Bxd8 Bb4+ {[%cal Gb4e1] Check, and White has only one way to block.}
8. Qd2 Kxd8 {[%cal Gb4d2] Black takes the bishop back. White's queen is pinned to the king and can't escape, so Black wins it next and ends up a knight ahead for a pawn. Stockfish: −5.6, winning for Black.} *

[Chapter "Take back with the bishop"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "7.Bxf6"]

{After 4.Bg5 Be7 5.e3 O-O 6.Nf3 h6, about one White player in seven takes on f6 at once with 7.Bxf6. Take back with the bishop: 7...Bxf6 keeps the pawns in front of the king whole and gives Black the bishop pair. The board shows the wrong recapture.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bxf6
7... gxf6? {Wrecks the pawns in front of Black's king.}
8. cxd5 exd5 {The material is level, but Black's king is open.}
9. Bd3 {[%cal Gd3h7] The bishop aims at h7, next to Black's king, which has lost its pawn cover. Stockfish: +2.9, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Take back with the knight"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "8.cxd5"]

{In the Tartakower, after 7...b6 8.cxd5, take back with the knight: 8...Nxd5 trades pieces (see Plans in the Tartakower). The tempting 8...c5?, which hits White's center, loses a pawn to a capture en passant.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 b6 8. cxd5
8... c5? {Hits d4, but the c-pawn passes c6, right next to White's pawn on d5.}
9. dxc6 {[%csl Yc6] White takes en passant: the pawn on d5 takes on c6, as if the c-pawn had moved one square. White has won a pawn, and Black's center is gone.} Nxc6 {Black is a pawn down. Stockfish: +1.8, clearly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Take back with the pawn"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "13.dxc5"]

{In the Tartakower, after 8.cxd5 Nxd5 9.Bxe7 Qxe7 10.Nxd5 exd5 11.Rc1 Be6 12.Bd3 c5 13.dxc5, take back with the pawn: 13...bxc5 gives Black hanging pawns. Before you take back with a piece, check which White pieces see the square. Here the rook on c1 does.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 b6 8. cxd5 Nxd5 9. Bxe7 Qxe7 10. Nxd5 exd5 11. Rc1 Be6 12. Bd3 c5 13. dxc5
13... Qxc5?? {[%cal Rc1c5] The queen takes back on the c-file, where White's rook stands.}
14. Rxc5 bxc5 {White has won the queen for a rook. Stockfish: +7.5, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Meeting the Exchange"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "ideas"]

{After 4.cxd5 exd5 the pawns are fixed: White's d4 and e3 against Black's d5 and c6. The trade frees your c8 bishop and gives you the half-open e-file. Your setup is ...Nbd7, ...c6, ...Be7, castling, ...Re8 and ...Nf8, where the knight guards h7 and can go on to e6 or g6. Stockfish rates the position on the first two boards as equal (+0.2).}
*

[Chapter "Against f3 and e4"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "10...Nf8"]

{This position comes from 4.cxd5 exd5 5.Bg5 Nbd7 6.e3 c6 7.Bd3 Be7 8.Nge2 O-O 9.Qc2 Re8 10.O-O Nf8. With the knight on e2, White's most common plan is f3 and e4, to open the center. Get ready with ...g6 and ...Ne6.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 Nbd7 6. e3 c6 7. Bd3 Be7 8. Nge2 O-O 9. Qc2 Re8 10. O-O Nf8 {[%cal Rf2f3,Re3e4]}
11. f3 {[%cal Re3e4] Takes e4 away from Black's knights and prepares e4.} g6 {[%csl Yh7] Shuts the d3 bishop out of h7, so the f8 knight can leave.}
12. Bh4 Ne6 {[%cal Ge6d4,Ge6g5] The knight hits d4 and g5, and Black is ready for e4. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Against the minority attack"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "10...Nf8"]

{Against Rb1, b4 and b5, don't open lines on the queenside for White. ...a6 makes White spend a move on a4 before b5. Meanwhile play on the kingside, where the f6 knight can jump to g4.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 Nbd7 6. e3 c6 7. Bd3 Be7 8. Nge2 O-O 9. Qc2 Re8 10. O-O Nf8 {[%cal Rb2b4,Rb4b5]}
11. Rab1 {[%cal Rb2b4] The minority attack begins.} g6 {Again ...g6 first, so the f8 knight can leave.}
12. b4 Ne6 {[%cal Ge6d4,Ge6g5] The knight hits d4 and g5.}
13. Bh4 a6 {[%cal Ra2a4] Now b5 needs a4 first.}
14. a4 Ng4 {[%cal Ge7h4] The knight jumps to g4, and the e7 bishop now attacks White's bishop on h4. Black has play on the kingside. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "When White's knight is on f3"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "10...Nf8"]

{Here White played 8.Nf3 instead of 8.Nge2, and 8...O-O 9.Qc2 Re8 10.O-O Nf8 followed. The knight blocks the f-pawn, so there is no f3-and-e4 plan, and White usually goes for the minority attack. Then ...Ne4 trades pieces, and the c8 bishop can come out to g4. Stockfish rates the position +0.5, slightly better for White.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 c6 6. e3 Be7 7. Bd3 Nbd7 8. Nf3 O-O 9. Qc2 Re8 10. O-O Nf8
11. Rab1 {[%cal Rb2b4] The minority attack begins.} Ne4 {[%cal Ge4c3,Ge7g5] The knight offers a trade, and the e7 bishop now hits White's bishop on g5.}
12. Bxe7 Qxe7 {One pair of bishops is gone.}
13. b4 Nxc3 {A pair of knights goes too.}
14. Qxc3 Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] The c8 bishop comes out and hits the knight on f3. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Exchange: 5...Be7"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation, Reshevsky Variation"]
[ECO "D36"]

{White's main line in this course. After 4.cxd5 exd5 5.Bg5, Black's most common reply is 5...Be7, in half of these games. White develops with e3, Bd3 and Qc2, brings the king's knight to e2 and castles short. The knight on e2 leaves the f-pawn free, so this chapter shows the f3 and e4 plan. Exchange: 5...c6 shows the minority attack.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 Be7 {Breaks the pin, so the knight on f6 can move again.}
6. e3 {Opens the diagonal of the f1 bishop.

6...Bf5?! develops the bishop, but it leaves b7 without a guard. 7.Bxf6 Bxf6 8.Qb3 hits b7 and d5 together. After 8...c6 9.Qxb7 Qb6 10.Qxb6 axb6, White is a pawn up in the endgame (Stockfish +1.0, clearly better for White).

Two common replies lead back to familiar positions. 6...c6 7.Qc2 is Exchange: 5...c6 after its 7.e3 (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White). 6...h6 7.Bh4 keeps the pin (Stockfish +0.3): after 7...O-O 8.Bd3 you are back in this line, and after 7...c6, develop the same way with Bd3, Qc2, Nge2 and castling.} O-O
7. Bd3 {[%cal Gd3h7,Gd3f5] The bishop aims at h7 and covers f5, the best square for Black's c8 bishop.

Against 7...c5, play 8.Nge2. After 8...cxd4 9.Nxd4, Black is left with an isolated pawn on d5 (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).} h6 {Asks the bishop to decide. Against Black's other seventh moves, keep developing with Qc2, Nge2 and castling.}
8. Bh4 {Keeps the pin. The bishop can drop back to g3 later.} c6 {Holds d5 and gives the queen a way out to b6 or a5.}
9. Qc2 {[%cal Gc2h7] This is the Reshevsky Variation. The queen joins the bishop on the diagonal to h7 and keeps Black's bishop off f5.

9...Be6 is the other main move. Play 10.Nge2 and the same plan: castle, then f3 and e4 (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).} Nbd7
10. Nge2 {[%cal Gf2f3] The knight goes to e2, not f3, so the f-pawn can move.} Re8 11. O-O Nf8 {[%cal Gf8g6,Gf8e6] The knight guards h7 and heads for g6 or e6.}
12. f3 {[%cal Ge3e4] Takes e4 and g4 away from Black's knights and prepares e4. Bring a rook to e1 or d1 first, then push e4 when the pieces are ready. The game is level, but White's plan is clear and Black has to find active moves. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Exchange: 4.cxd5"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation, Reshevsky Variation"]
[ECO "D36"]

{About one White player in six takes on d5 at once. The pawns are fixed, and Black's c8 bishop is free. Play 5...Nbd7: it sets the Elephant trap, which catches one White player in five (see the trap chapter). Then ...c6, ...Be7, castle, ...Re8 and ...Nf8. The plans are on Key ideas: Meeting the Exchange.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3
(3. cxd5 exd5 4. Nc3 Nf6 {The same position as after 4.cxd5 exd5 in the main line. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.})
3... Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 {[%cal Gc8f5] The c8 bishop's diagonal is open again.}
5. Bg5 {[%cal Gg5f6,Rf6d5] Pins the knight that guards d5. Against 5.Nf3 or 5.Bf4, see Advanced variations.} Nbd7 {[%csl Yd5] Now d5 looks loose, but 6.Nxd5?? loses a piece to 6...Nxd5!: see the trap chapter on the Elephant trap.}
6. e3 {Against 6.Nf3, play 6...c6 and the same setup.} c6 {[%cal Gc6d5] Guards d5 for good.}
7. Bd3 Be7 8. Nge2 {The most common move. 8.Qc2 O-O 9.Nge2 reaches the same position. Against 8.Nf3, use the same setup: ...O-O, ...Re8 and ...Nf8 (Stockfish +0.5, slightly better for White).} O-O
9. Qc2 Re8 10. O-O {This is the Reshevsky Variation.} Nf8 {[%cal Gf8g6,Gf8e6] The knight guards h7 and heads for e6 or g6.}
11. f3 {[%cal Ge3e4] White's most common plan: prepare e4. Against 11.Rab1 and the minority attack, see Key ideas: Meeting the Exchange.} g6 {[%cal Gf8e6] Prepares ...Ne6.}
12. Bh4 Ne6 {[%cal Ge6d4,Ge6g5] The knight hits d4 and g5, and Black is ready to meet e4. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Other third moves"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "3.a3"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined"]
[ECO "D30"]

{Rarer third moves. Against 3.a3, which stops ...Bb4, play 3...Nf6 and the usual setup. After 3.cxd5 exd5 4.Nf3, develop the same way. 3.e3 usually reaches Three Knights: 5.e3. Against 3.g3, 3...Nf6 4.Bg2 Be7 5.Nf3 O-O reaches the Catalan; against 3.Bf4, 3...Nf6 4.e3 c5 hits the center (Stockfish 0.0, equal).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. a3
(3. cxd5 exd5 4. Nf3 Nf6 {Develop with ...Be7 or ...Bd6, ...c6 and castle. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
3... Nf6 4. e3 Be7 {Castle next, as usual. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Tartakower: 8.cxd5"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Tartakower Defense, Makogonov Exchange Variation"]
[ECO "D59"]

{Black's main line in this course. After 4.Bg5 Be7 5.e3 O-O 6.Nf3 h6 7.Bh4 b6, four White players in ten take on d5 at once. Take back with the knight and trade two pairs of minor pieces: every trade gives Black's pieces more room. Then ...Be6 guards d5, and ...c5 hits White's center. Against castling, ...c4 gains space. If White takes on c5, take back with the pawn and play with hanging pawns (see Key ideas: The hanging pawns).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 {If White starts with 3.Nf3 Nf6 4.Bg5, about one game in thirty, play 4...Be7 and castle. Seven games in ten then reach this chapter: 5.Nc3 O-O is the 5.Nf3 branch below, and 5.e3 O-O 6.Nc3 h6 is the main line after 6...h6. If White plays Bd3 and castles before Nc3, play ...h6, ...b6 and ...Bb7 all the same: 6.Bd3 h6 7.Bh4 b6 8.O-O Bb7 9.Nc3 is Tartakower: 8.Bd3 after 9.O-O (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Against 6.Nbd2, 6...h6 7.Bh4 b6 (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 {Breaks the pin and prepares to castle. Against 5.cxd5 or 5.Bxf6, see Advanced variations.}
5. e3
(5. Nf3 O-O 6. e3 h6 {The same position as in the main line after 6...h6. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
5... O-O
6. Nf3 {The most common move. Against 6.Bd3 or 6.cxd5, see Tartakower: 6.Bd3 and 6.cxd5. After 6.cxd5, take back at once: 6...h6? drops a pawn. Against 6.Rc1 or 6.Qc2, play 6...h6 and ...b6 just the same. If 6.Qc2 h6 7.h4, White aims at Black's king: hit back in the center with 7...c5 (Stockfish −0.2, equal).} h6 {[%cal Rh6g5] Puts the question to the bishop. Against 7.Bxf6, see Tartakower: 7.Bxf6.}
7. Bh4 {Against 7.Bf4, about one game in fourteen, strike in the center with 7...c5, as in Three Knights: 5.Bf4. After 8.cxd5 Nxd5 9.Nxd5, take back with the queen: 9...Qxd5 10.dxc5 Qxc5 (Stockfish −0.2, equal). 9...cxd4?? loses a piece to 10.Nxe7+ (Stockfish +6.7).} b6 {[%cal Gc8b7,Gc7c5] The Tartakower: the bishop comes out next, then ...c5.}
8. cxd5 Nxd5 {This is the Makogonov Exchange Variation. Take back with the knight: it offers trades.}
(8... c5? {Natural, but White's pawn on d5 can take it en passant.} 9. dxc6 {White has won a pawn, and Black's center is gone. Stockfish: +1.8, clearly better for White.})
9. Bxe7 Qxe7 {After 9.Nxd5 exd5 10.Bxe7 Qxe7, the same position arises.}
10. Nxd5 exd5 {[%csl Yd5] The c8 bishop is free again, and Black has the half-open e-file.}
11. Bd3 {The most common move. 11.Rc1 is under Advanced variations.} Be6 {[%cal Ge6d5] The bishop guards d5. With the pawn fixed on d5, a bishop on b7 would bite on its own pawn.}
12. O-O c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Black's freeing break.}
13. Rc1 {The most common move. After 13.dxc5 bxc5, Black has active hanging pawns on c5 and d5 (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} c4 {[%cal Gc4b3] Gains space on the queenside and drives the bishop back.}
14. Bb1 Nc6 {The last minor piece comes out. If 15.Ne5, trade it with 15...Nxe5. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "Exchange: 4...Nxd5"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Semi-Tarrasch Defense, Exchange Variation"]
[ECO "D41"]

{About one player in four takes back on d5 with the knight. Black keeps a flexible pawn structure, but the knight can be chased. Answer with 5.e4: White gains time and builds a full center. Black's best is to trade knights and hit the center with ...c5 at once.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 Nxd5
5. e4 {[%cal Ge4d5] Hits the knight and takes the center.

If the knight retreats, with 5...Nf6 or 5...Nb6, then 6.Nf3 gives White a big center for free (Stockfish +1.4, clearly better for White). 5...Bb4?? loses a piece to 6.exd5 (Stockfish +6.4, winning for White).} Nxc3
6. bxc3 {[%csl Yc3,Yd4,Ye4] White's pawns on c3, d4 and e4 form a strong center, and the b-file is half-open for a rook.} c5 {[%cal Rc5d4] The right way: Black hits d4 at once. 6...Be7 is slower, and White keeps a bigger edge after 7.Nf3 (Stockfish +0.7, slightly better for White).}
7. Nf3 {This is the Semi-Tarrasch Defense, Exchange Variation. The knight guards d4.

7...Nc6? is a mistake: 8.d5! drives the knight away, and White's pawns roll forward (Stockfish +2.2, winning for White).} cxd4 8. cxd4 Bb4+ 9. Bd2 Bxd2+ {Trading pieces eases Black's defense, because Black has less space.} 10. Qxd2 O-O 11. Bc4 {[%cal Gc4f7] The bishop takes an active diagonal, aimed at f7.} b6 12. O-O Bb7 13. Rfe1 {[%cal Gd4d5,Ge1e4] White has the bigger center, and d5 is the break: when the pawn advances, lines open toward Black's king. Black wants to blockade with pieces and attack d4 with ...Nc6 and ...Rc8. Stockfish: +0.4, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Exchange: 5...c6"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation, Reshevsky Variation"]
[ECO "D36"]

{Black's second most common reply, in about a quarter of these games. Black guards d5 first and keeps the f8 bishop's choice open. Play 6.Qc2 at once: it stops ...Bf5, Black's most natural way to free the c8 bishop. Then e3, Bd3, Nge2 and castle, and start the minority attack with Rab1 and b4.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 c6
6. Qc2 {[%cal Gc2f5] This is the Reshevsky Variation. The queen covers f5, so the c8 bishop can't come out there yet.

Black can prepare ...Bf5 with ...g6. After 6...g6 7.e3 Bf5 8.Bd3 Bxd3 9.Qxd3, or 6...h6 7.Bh4 g6 8.e3 Bf5 9.Bd3 Bxd3 10.Qxd3, the light-squared bishops are traded and the game is level (Stockfish 0.0 and +0.1, equal). Against 6...Bd6, play 7.e3 and develop as usual (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).} Be7
7. e3 {7...Nh5? looks like a good way to trade off White's bishop, but it leaves c8 without a guard: see the trap chapter on 7...Nh5.} O-O 8. Bd3 Nbd7 9. Nge2 Re8 10. O-O Nf8
11. Rab1 {[%cal Gb2b4,Gb4b5] The minority attack begins. Next b4 and b5, then bxc6, to leave Black with a weak pawn on c6 or d5. Black's chances lie on the kingside, with ...Ne4 or ...Ng6. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Exchange: 5...Nbd7 and 5...Bb4"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "5...Nbd7"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation, Reshevsky Variation"]
[ECO "D36"]

{Two rarer fifth moves, each in about one Exchange game in twelve. 5...Nbd7 sets the Elephant trap: don't grab d5, and develop as usual with e3, Bd3, Qc2 and Nge2. 5...Bb4 pins the knight on c3: castle quickly and keep d4 well guarded. 5...Be6, in about one game in twenty, is met the same way: 6.e3, then Bd3 and Nge2 (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 Nbd7 {[%csl Yd5] The pawn on d5 looks loose, because the knight on f6 is pinned.}
(5... Bb4 {Pins the knight on c3.} 6. e3 {Against 6...h6, keep the pin with 7.Bh4 and develop the same way.} O-O 7. Bd3 {Castle next, and keep d4 well guarded. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.})
6. e3 {Develop. With the knight on d7, taking on d5 loses a piece.}
(6. Nxd5?? {The Elephant trap. White wins a pawn but loses a piece.} Nxd5! 7. Bxd8 Bb4+ 8. Qd2 Bxd2+ 9. Kxd2 Kxd8 {Black has won a knight for a pawn. Stockfish: −5.1, winning for Black.})
6... c6 {The most common reply. After 6...Be7, 7.Bd3 c6 8.Qc2 reaches the same position.}
7. Bd3 Be7 8. Qc2 {This is the Reshevsky Variation.} O-O 9. Nge2 Re8 10. O-O Nf8 {The same position as in Exchange: 5...c6 after 10...Nf8. There we played the minority attack; here is the other plan.}
11. f3 {[%cal Ge3e4] Takes e4 away from Black's knights and prepares e4. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Exchange: 5.Nf3 and 5.Bf4"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "5.Nf3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation"]
[ECO "D35"]

{Without the pin on f6, Black can bring the c8 bishop to f5. Play ...c6 to guard d5, then ...Be7 and ...Bf5 (or ...Bf5 at once against 5.Bf4), and castle.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5
5. Nf3
(5. Bf4 c6 6. e3 Bf5 {[%cal Gf5d3] Offers to trade Black's problem bishop.} 7. Bd3 Bxd3 8. Qxd3 Be7 {The light-squared bishops are gone. Castle next. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
5... c6 6. Bg5 {Almost six White players in ten. Against 6.Bf4, 6...Bf5 7.e3 Be7 reaches the end of Three Knights: 5.cxd5 and 5.g3 (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Against 6.e3, put the bishop on f5 at once, and trade it if White offers: 6...Bf5 7.Bd3 Bxd3 8.Qxd3 Bd6 (Stockfish −0.3, slightly better for Black). Against 6.g3, 6...Bf5 too (Stockfish −0.1, equal).} Be7 7. e3 Bf5 {[%cal Gf5d3] The bishop takes its best square, since no White queen covers f5. If 8.Nh4, retreat to e6. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Semi-Slav: 3...c6"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Semi-Slav Defense: Accelerated Move Order"]
[ECO "D31"]

{About one Black player in nine plays 3...c6, keeping the choice between Semi-Slav and Slav setups open. Take on d5 anyway. Without a knight on f6 there is nothing to pin, so the bishop goes to f4 instead of g5. 3...dxc4 is rarer: 4.e4 takes the center, and after 4...Bb4 5.Bxc4 White is slightly better (Stockfish +0.7).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 c6 {This is the Semi-Slav Defense, Accelerated Move Order.}
4. cxd5 {Fixes the pawns before Black can choose.} exd5 {Six players in ten take back with the e-pawn, and the Carlsbad structure appears.}
(4... cxd5 {A symmetrical structure.} 5. Bf4 {[%cal Gf4b8] The bishop takes the open diagonal.} Nc6 6. e3 Nf6 7. Bd3 {Next Nf3 or Nge2, then castle. White's bishops are more active. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.})
5. Bf4 {[%cal Gf4b8] With no knight on f6 to pin, the bishop goes to f4. Against 5...Bd6, trade with 6.Bxd6 Qxd6 7.e3 (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} Nf6
6. e3 {Next Bd3, Nf3 or Nge2, castle, and the minority attack, as in the Exchange. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "3...Bb4"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Queen's Knight Variation"]
[ECO "D31"]

{3...Bb4 pins the knight at once, in about one game in eleven. Take on d5, as in the Exchange. After 4...exd5 5.Nf3 Nf6 6.Bg5 the game looks like the Exchange Variation, with Black's bishop on b4.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4
4. cxd5 {Two other recaptures are common. After 4...Bxc3+ 5.bxc3 exd5, 6.Ba3 keeps Black's king from castling for now, and White has the bishop pair (Stockfish +0.7, slightly better for White). After 4...Qxd5, 5.e3 and Nf3 develop, and the queen will be chased (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White).} exd5
5. Nf3 Nf6 6. Bg5 {The same pin as in the Exchange.} h6 7. Bxf6 Qxf6 8. e3 c6
9. Bd3 {Castle next, then the minority attack or e4. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "Three Knights: 5.Bf4"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Harrwitz Attack"]
[ECO "D37"]

{After 4.Nf3 Be7, about three White players in ten put the bishop on f4 instead of g5. Without a pin, Black can hit the center at once with ...c5. Against 7.cxd5, take back with the knight and trade into an equal position with no weaknesses. Against 5.Bg5, play 5...O-O 6.e3 h6 and the Tartakower chapters.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3
(3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 Be7 {The same position as after 4.Nf3 Be7 in the main line. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.})
3... Nf6 4. Nf3 {Against 4.Bf4, play 4...Be7 and castle. After 5.Nf3 O-O, or 5.e3 O-O 6.Nf3 c5, you are in this chapter. Against 5.c5, see Harrwitz Attack: 4.Bf4.} Be7 5. Bf4 {This is the Harrwitz Attack.} O-O
6. e3 {6.Nb5?! hits c7, but after 6...a6 7.Nc3 dxc4 Black has gained time and taken the c4 pawn (Stockfish −1.7, clearly better for Black).} c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Strikes at d4 before White has castled.}
7. cxd5 {The most common move. Against 7.dxc5, take back with the bishop: 7...Bxc5 frees the game (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White). Against 7.Bd3 or 7.Be2, 7...cxd4 releases the tension (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} Nxd5
8. Nxd5 {Against 8.Bg3, pin the knight with 8...Qa5 (Stockfish −0.4, slightly better for Black).} Qxd5 {Take back with the queen.}
(8... cxd4?? {Looks like a good trade, but the knight on d5 takes on e7 with check.} 9. Nxe7+ Qxe7 10. Qxd4 {White has won a piece. Stockfish: +6.5, winning for White.})
9. dxc5 Qxc5 {Black has no weaknesses and will develop with ...Nc6, ...Rd8 and ...Bd7. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Three Knights: 5.e3"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Three Knights Variation"]
[ECO "D37"]

{After 4.Nf3 Be7, about one White player in five plays the quiet 5.e3. The same position often comes from 3.e3 or 4.e3. Castle, wait for White's bishop to move, then take on c4 and play ...c5, as in the Queen's Gambit Accepted.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3
(3. e3 Nf6 4. Nf3 {Against 4.cxd5 exd5, develop with ...Be7, castle and play ...c6, as in Three Knights: 6.cxd5.} Be7 5. Nc3 {Against 5.Bd3, castle: 6.Nc3 is the main line after 6.Bd3. If 6.O-O, take on c4 now that the bishop has moved: 6...dxc4 7.Bxc4 c5 (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} O-O {The same position as in the main line after 5...O-O. If White plays 4.Nc3 first, 4...Be7 5.Nf3 O-O gets there too. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
3... Nf6 4. Nf3
(4. e3 Be7 5. Nf3 {Two White players in three. Against 5.Bd3, castle: 6.Nf3 is the main line after 6.Bd3. If 6.Nge2, take on c4 now that the bishop has moved: 6...dxc4 7.Bxc4 c5 (Stockfish −0.2, equal). Against 5.cxd5 exd5, castle and play ...c6: 6.Bd3 O-O 7.Nf3 c6 is Three Knights: 6.cxd5.} O-O {The same position as in the main line after 5...O-O. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
4... Be7 5. e3 O-O
6. Bd3 {The most common move. Against 6.Be2, take on c4 just the same: 6...dxc4 7.Bxc4 is this line again, nine times in ten. If White castles first, 7.O-O c5 8.Bxc4 a6 reaches the end of this line too (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Against 6.cxd5, take back with the pawn: see Three Knights: 6.cxd5. 6...c5? runs into 7.dxc6 (Stockfish +1.4).} dxc4 {White's bishop has just moved, so Bxc4 costs it a second move.}
7. Bxc4 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] The freeing break.}
8. O-O a6 {[%cal Gb7b5] Prepares ...b5, which gains time on the bishop. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Three Knights: 5.cxd5 and 5.g3"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "5.cxd5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Three Knights Variation"]
[ECO "D37"]

{Two rarer fifth moves after 4.Nf3 Be7. After 5.cxd5 exd5 6.Bf4, play ...c6 and ...Bf5, as against the Exchange; against 6.Bg5, 6...c6 7.e3 Bf5 reaches Exchange: 5.Nf3. After 5.g3, castle and take on c4: a Catalan in which White's knight is already on c3.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 Be7
5. cxd5
(5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 dxc4 7. O-O Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4] The knight hits d4. Stockfish: −0.2, equal.})
5... exd5 6. Bf4 c6 7. e3 Bf5 {[%cal Gf5d3] The same setup as in the Exchange. Castle next. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Three Knights: 6.cxd5"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Three Knights Variation"]
[ECO "D37"]

{After 4.Nf3 Be7 5.e3 O-O, about one White player in eight takes on d5. The same pawns can come from 4.e3 Be7 5.cxd5 and from 3.e3 Nf6 4.cxd5. Take back with the pawn: no White bishop pins your knight, and ...exd5 opens the c8 bishop's diagonal. Then ...c6 holds d5, and the bishop comes out: to g4 against Bd3, to f5 against Be2.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. cxd5 exd5 {[%cal Gc8g4] Take back with the pawn, as three club players in four do. It opens the c8 bishop's diagonal. 6...Nxd5 is fine too (Stockfish +0.1, equal), but it leaves the bishop shut in.}
7. Bd3 {Two White players in three.}
(7. Be2 {Almost one White player in five.} c6 8. O-O Bf5 {[%cal Gf5b1] With White's bishop on e2, no White piece covers f5, and the bishop takes its best square. If 9.Nh4, retreat to e6. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.})
7... c6 {[%cal Gc6d5] Guards d5 for good. The pawns now stand as in the Exchange Variation (see Key ideas: Meeting the Exchange), but here the c8 bishop can still come out.}
8. O-O {Two White players in three. Against 8.h3, which takes g4 away from the bishop, or 8.Qc2, play 8...Re8 and ...Bd6 (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Pins the knight on f3. The bishop is active outside the pawn chain, and half of club players choose this move. Quiet moves such as 8...Nbd7 or 8...Re8 are just as good (Stockfish +0.1, equal).}
9. h3 {Two White players in three.} Bh5 {[%cal Gh5f3] Keeps the pin. If 10.Qb3 hits b7, 10...Bxf3 11.gxf3 Qd7 guards it, and the pawns in front of White's king are doubled. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "Catalan: 4.g3"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Catalan Opening: Open Defense, Classical Line"]
[ECO "E05"]

{3.Nf3 comes up in about one game in five, and after 3...Nf6 about one White player in four plays 4.g3, the Catalan. The bishop on g2 aims at b7 and the queenside. Develop with ...Be7 and castle, then take on c4 once White has castled. Use ...a6, then ...b5 and ...Bb7 or ...Bd7 and ...Bc6, to blunt the g2 bishop.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. g3 {This is the Catalan Opening.} Be7 5. Bg2 O-O
6. O-O dxc4 {[%csl Yc4] White has castled, so Black takes: the pawn is hard to win back at once, and White has to spend time on it.}
7. Qc2 {The most common move, with 7.Ne5 (under Advanced variations). The queen wins the pawn back next.} a6 {[%cal Gb7b5] Threatens ...b5, which would hold the pawn.}
8. a4 {Stops ...b5. Against 8.Qxc4, play 8...b5 9.Qc2 Bb7: the bishop opposes White's on g2 (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} Bd7 {[%cal Gd7c6] Heads for c6, where it blunts White's bishop on the long diagonal.}
9. Qxc4 Bc6 10. Bg5 Nbd7 {Black has finished development, and the bishop on c6 holds the long diagonal. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Catalan: 7.Ne5"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Catalan Opening: Open Defense, Classical Line"]
[ECO "E05"]

{About one Catalan player in three wins the pawn back with 7.Ne5. Hit the knight with 7...Nc6: after the trades on c6, material is level and Black's pieces are free.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. g3 Be7 5. Bg2 O-O 6. O-O dxc4
7. Ne5 Nc6 {[%cal Gc6e5] Challenges the knight.}
8. Bxc6
(8. Nxc6 bxc6 {Take back with the pawn. Black keeps the extra pawn on c4. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.})
8... bxc6 9. Nxc6 Qe8 {[%cal Ge8c6] Hits the knight.}
10. Nxe7+ Qxe7 {Material is level, White has given up the g2 bishop, and Black's pieces are free. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Tartakower: 5.cxd5 and 5.Bxf6"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "5.cxd5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation, Positional Variation"]
[ECO "D35"]

{Two fifth-move tries, each in about one game in eighteen. After 5.cxd5 exd5, the pawns are the same as in the Exchange Variation: play ...c6 and ...Nbd7 and use the same plans. After 5.Bxf6 Bxf6, Black has the bishop pair: castle and hit the center with ...c5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7
5. cxd5
(5. Bxf6 {This is the Heral Variation.} Bxf6 6. e3 {Against 6.cxd5, take back with the e-pawn: 6...exd5 7.e3 c6 (Stockfish −0.1, equal).} O-O 7. Nf3 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4,Gf6d4] The bishop on f6 supports the break. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
5... exd5 6. e3 {Two White players in three. Against 6.Nf3, play 6...c6: 7.e3 Bf5 reaches the end of Exchange: 5.Nf3 and 5.Bf4 (Stockfish +0.1, equal). If 7.Qc2 keeps the bishop off f5, prepare it with 7...g6 and ...Bf5 (Stockfish 0.0, equal). Against 6.Qc2, play 6...c6 and the Exchange setup (Stockfish +0.2, equal).} c6 7. Bd3 Nbd7 {Next ...Nf8 and ...Ne6, or castle, as in the Exchange chapter. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Tartakower: 6.Bd3 and 6.cxd5"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "6.Bd3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined"]
[ECO "D53"]

{After 4.Bg5 Be7 5.e3 O-O, about one White player in ten plays 6.Bd3 before Nf3, and about one in seventeen takes on d5 at once with 6.cxd5. Against 6.Bd3, keep the Tartakower setup: ...h6, ...b6 and ...Bb7. Then take on c4 once White's bishop has moved. Against 6.cxd5, take back at once with the knight and trade pieces, as against 8.cxd5 in the main line. Here the usual ...h6 would drop a pawn.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O
6. Bd3 {White develops the bishop before the king's knight. Play ...h6 and ...b6 as usual. If White's knight goes to f3 later, you are often back in the main Tartakower chapters.}
(6. cxd5 Nxd5 {Take back with the knight. It offers trades, and every trade gives Black's pieces more room. Most club players take back with the e-pawn, which is also playable (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White), but the knight keeps the plan of the main line.}
(6... h6? {White has just taken a pawn, and Black must take it back now.} 7. Bxf6 Bxf6 8. dxe6 Bxe6 {The knight that could have taken back on d5 is gone, and White's pawn took a second pawn on e6. White is a pawn up. Stockfish: +1.2, clearly better for White.})
(6... c5? {The same trick as 8...c5? in the main line: White's pawn on d5 takes it en passant.} 7. dxc6 {White has won a pawn. Stockfish: +1.3, clearly better for White.})
7. Bxe7 Qxe7
8. Bd3 {The most common move, about four times in ten. Against 8.Nxd5 exd5, the c8 bishop is free again, as in the main line (Stockfish 0.0, equal). Against 8.Nf3, trade knights with 8...Nxc3 9.bxc3, then ...b6 and ...Bb7 (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} Nxc3 {Trades a second pair of pieces.}
9. bxc3 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits White's center before White has castled.}
10. Nf3 b6 11. O-O Bb7 {[%cal Gb7f3] The Tartakower setup after all. Next come ...Nd7 and ...Rc8. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
6... h6 7. Bh4 {Three White players in four. Against 7.Bxf6 Bxf6 8.Nf3, you are in Tartakower: 7.Bxf6. Against 7.Bf4, hit the center with 7...c5 (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} b6 {[%cal Gc8b7] The bishop goes to b7 next.}
8. Nge2 {One White player in four puts the knight on e2, where it doesn't block the f-pawn. The most common move, 8.Nf3, reaches Tartakower: 8.Bd3. Against 8.cxd5, take back with the knight: 8...Nxd5 9.Bxe7 Qxe7 10.Nxd5 exd5, then ...c5, as in the main line (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} Bb7
9. O-O {Seven times in ten.} dxc4 {White's bishop has already moved once, so Bxc4 costs it a second move.}
10. Bxc4 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4,Gb7g2] Black hits the center, and the bishop on b7 looks down the long diagonal. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Tartakower: 8.Bd3"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Tartakower Defense"]
[ECO "D58"]

{About one White player in four develops with 8.Bd3. Finish development with ...Bb7 and ...Nbd7, then take on c4: White's bishop has already moved once, so Bxc4 costs White a tempo. Then ...c5 frees the game.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 b6
8. Bd3 Bb7 {[%cal Gb7d5] The bishop goes to the long diagonal, behind the pawn on d5.}
9. O-O {Against 9.cxd5, take back with the knight: 9...Nxd5 trades pieces (Stockfish −0.1, equal).} Nbd7
10. Rc1 {The most common move. Against 10.Qe2, play 10...c5: after 11.Rfd1 or 11.Rad1, 11...Ne4 trades pieces (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Against 10.cxd5, 10...exd5 is the simple recapture (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).} dxc4 {White's bishop moved to d3, and now it must move again to take back.}
11. Bxc4 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4,Gb7g2] Black hits the center, and the bishop on b7 eyes the long diagonal. Next ...cxd4 and ...a6. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Tartakower: other eighth moves"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "8.Be2"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Tartakower Defense"]
[ECO "D58"]

{Three quieter eighth moves, each in about one game in ten: 8.Be2, 8.Rc1 and 8.Qc2. Play ...Bb7 and ...Nbd7. If White takes on d5, take back with the knight and trade. Then break with ...c5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 b6
8. Be2
(8. Rc1 Bb7 9. cxd5 {The most common follow-up.} Nxd5 10. Bxe7 Qxe7 {Two pairs of pieces will come off, as in the main line. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
(8. Qc2 Bb7 9. cxd5 Nxd5 10. Bxe7 Qxe7 {The same idea: trade pieces, then ...c5. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.})
8... Bb7 9. O-O Nbd7 10. Rc1 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Black's freeing break. If White takes on d5, take back with the knight. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "Tartakower: 7.Bxf6"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Anti-Tartakower Variation"]
[ECO "D55"]

{About one White player in seven takes on f6 before Black can play ...b6. Take back with the bishop: Black gets the bishop pair, and the bishop on f6 presses on d4. Wait for White's bishop to move, then take on c4 and play ...c5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6
7. Bxf6 {This is the Anti-Tartakower Variation.} Bxf6 {Always take back with the bishop.}
(7... gxf6? {Wrecks the pawns in front of Black's king.} 8. cxd5 exd5 9. Bd3 {[%cal Gd3h7,Gd1c2,Gf3h4] White's pieces aim at the open king, and Qc2 or Nh4 comes next. Stockfish: +2.9, winning for White.})
8. Bd3 {The most common move.

Against 8.cxd5, take back with the e-pawn: 8...exd5, then ...c6, ...Be6 and ...Nd7 (Stockfish +0.1, equal). 8...c5? runs into 9.dxc6, the same trick as in the main Tartakower (Stockfish +1.0). Against 8.Rc1, play 8...c6 and ...Nd7 (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} dxc4 {White's bishop has just moved, so Bxc4 costs it a second move.}
9. Bxc4 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4,Gf6d4] The freeing break. The bishop on f6 adds pressure on d4.}
10. O-O cxd4 {After 11.exd4, White has an isolated pawn on d4, and Black's bishop on f6 hits it. After 11.Nxd4, develop with ...Bd7 and ...Nc6. Stockfish: −0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Tartakower: 11.Rc1"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "11.Rc1"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Tartakower Defense"]
[ECO "D59"]

{About one White player in six puts the rook on the c-file at once. Play 11...Be6 and ...c5, as in the main line. After 13.dxc5, Black gets hanging pawns: take back with the pawn, never the queen. Against 12.Qa4, which eyes a7 and c6, 12...c5 is still the answer.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 b6 8. cxd5 Nxd5 9. Bxe7 Qxe7 10. Nxd5 exd5
11. Rc1 Be6
12. Bd3
(12. Qa4 c5 {Black's freeing break. Next ...Rc8 or ...a5. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
12... c5 13. dxc5 bxc5 {[%csl Yc5,Yd5] Hanging pawns on c5 and d5: they need care, but they control the center and can advance (see Key ideas: The hanging pawns). Stockfish: 0.0, equal.}
(13... Qxc5?? {Takes back on the c-file, where White's rook stands.} 14. Rxc5 {White wins the queen for a rook. Stockfish: +7.5, winning for White.}) *

[Chapter "Harrwitz Attack: 4.Bf4"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "5.c5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Harrwitz Attack"]
[ECO "D35"]

{About one game in thirty goes 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.Bf4. Play 4...Be7 and castle. About half the time White adds Nf3, and you are in Three Knights: 5.Bf4. This chapter covers the space grab c4–c5, which takes the ...c5 break away from Black. Hit the head of White's pawn chain at once with ...b6. When White holds c5 with b4, undermine that pawn with ...a5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bf4 {This is the Harrwitz Attack. The bishop comes out before e3, but unlike Bg5, it doesn't pin the knight on f6.} Be7 {Castle next. Stockfish's first choice is 4...c5 (0.0, equal), but 4...Be7 leads to Three Knights: 5.Bf4 in about half of games (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).}
5. c5 {[%cal Gc4c5] About one White player in nine grabs space on the queenside. The pawn on c5 stops ...c5, Black's usual freeing break. Against 5.Nf3 O-O, you are in Three Knights: 5.Bf4.}
(5. e3 {Almost six White players in ten.} O-O 6. c5 {One White player in twelve grabs space here. Against 6.Nf3, play 6...c5: you are in Three Knights: 5.Bf4. Against 6.Bd3 or 6.h3, play 6...c5 too (Stockfish 0.0 and +0.1, equal).} b6 {[%cal Gb6c5] Hits the head of the chain, as in the main line.} 7. b4 a5 8. a3 axb4 {The same position as in the main line after 8...axb4. Stockfish: −0.2, equal.})
5... O-O {Castle first. 5...b6 at once is just as good (Stockfish −0.3).}
6. e3 {The most common move, chosen by half of White players. Against 6.h3 or 6.Nf3, play 6...b6 just the same (Stockfish −0.5 and −0.3, slightly better for Black).} b6 {[%cal Gb6c5] Attacks the pawn on c5, the head of White's pawn chain.}
7. b4 {Nine White players in ten hold c5 with the b-pawn. After 7.cxb6 axb6, Black's rook gets the half-open a-file, and ...c5 comes next (Stockfish −0.3, slightly better for Black).} a5 {[%cal Ga5b4] Undermines the pawn on b4, which holds up c5.}
8. a3 axb4 9. axb4 Rxa1 {Trades rooks on the open a-file.}
10. Qxa1 Nc6 {[%cal Gc6b4,Ge6e5] The knight hits b4, and ...e5 comes next to strike at White's center. Stockfish: −0.4, slightly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "Trap: the Elephant trap"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "6.Nxd5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation, Positional Variation"]
[ECO "D35"]

{The most rewarding trap in this repertoire. In the Exchange Variation, after 5...Nbd7, the pawn on d5 looks free, because the knight on f6 is pinned. One White player in five grabs it. The pin isn't real: after 6...Nxd5!, White can take Black's queen, but ...Bb4+ wins it back, and Black ends up a piece ahead.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 Nbd7
6. Nxd5?? {Grabs the pawn.} Nxd5! {Gives up the queen.}
7. Bxd8 {White takes the queen. Other moves leave White a knight down.}
(7. Nf3 Bb4+ 8. Bd2 Bxd2+ 9. Qxd2 N7f6 {Black is a knight up for a pawn. Stockfish: −5.7, winning for Black.})
7... Bb4+ {[%cal Gb4e1] Check, and White has only one way to block.}
8. Qd2 Bxd2+ {[%engine ok] Takes the queen back. 8...Kxd8 is even stronger, because the queen stays pinned and can't escape.}
9. Kxd2 Kxd8 {Black has won a knight for a pawn. Stockfish: −5.1, winning for Black.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 7...Nh5? and Nxd5"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "7...Nh5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation, Reshevsky Variation"]
[ECO "D36"]

{In the Exchange Variation, Black often tries to trade off White's strong bishop with ...Nh5. Played before Black's knight has left b8, it loses a pawn: nothing guards c8, so the c6 pawn can't safely take back on d5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 c6 6. Qc2 Be7 7. e3 Nh5? {Gets out of the e7 bishop's way, so ...Bxg5 is threatened.}
8. Bxe7 Qxe7? {The natural recapture, but now nothing guards c8. 8...Kxe7 keeps the queen on d8, where it guards c8: Black's king can't castle any more, but no material is lost (Stockfish +1.1, clearly better for White).}
9. Nxd5! {[%cal Gc2c8] The c6 pawn can't take back safely: the c-file would open, and the queen on c2 takes the bishop on c8.} cxd5?
(9... Qd6 {The best defense: the queen hits the knight.} 10. Nc3 {The knight goes back. Black is a pawn down, and the knight on h5 is far from the center. Stockfish: +3.1, winning for White.})
10. Qxc8+ Qd8 11. Bb5+ Nc6 12. Bxc6+ bxc6 13. Qxc6+ {White is two pawns up, and the queen attacks the rook on a8 too. Stockfish: +5.5, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Map of the variations"]
[Type "map"]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 {Queen's Gambit Declined}
3. Nc3 (3. Nf3 Nf6 4. g3 {Catalan}) (3. a3 {[%course black] Other third moves})
3... Nf6 (3... c6 {Semi-Slav move order}) (3... Bb4 {})
4. cxd5 {[%course black] Exchange Variation} (4. Bg5 {Main line} Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 b6 {Tartakower} 8. cxd5 {[%course black]}) (4. Nf3 {Three Knights}) (4. Bf4 {Harrwitz Attack})
4... exd5 (4... Nxd5 {})
5. Bg5 Be7 {[%course white]} (5... c6 {Minority attack}) (5... Nbd7 {}) *
